        <h2 id="why-chimneys-fail" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Why Chimneys Fail
        </h2>

        <p>
          A chimney is the worst-treated masonry on a house. It stands above the roofline with weather hitting it from every direction, it has no eaves protecting it, and unlike a wall it is exposed on all four sides plus the top. On top of that, a working flue puts moisture and acidic combustion by-products inside the structure.
        </p>

        <p>
          The failure sequence is nearly always the same. Water enters the brick or the mortar joints, or comes in through a crack in the crown. In cold weather it freezes, expanding roughly nine per cent, and the pressure pops the face off the brick — spalling. Once the face is gone, the exposed interior of the brick is far more absorbent than the fired outer skin was, so it takes up more water, and the next winter does more damage than the last. Left long enough, the mortar joints wash out, the stack loses structural integrity, and the repair becomes a rebuild.
        </p>

        <p>
          Three entry points account for nearly all of it: the <strong>crown</strong> cracking, the <strong>brick and mortar</strong> absorbing water, and the <strong>flashing</strong> where the chimney meets the roof failing. A sealer addresses exactly one of those, which is why diagnosis comes before shopping.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Chimney Water Problem</p>
