        </div>

        <h2 id="breathability" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Why Breathability Is Everything
        </h2>

        <p>
          This is the section that matters more than the product choice, because getting it wrong makes the chimney worse rather than better.
        </p>

        <p>
          Masonry always contains some moisture, and a chimney contains more than most: water vapour from combustion, condensation in the flue, and whatever the brick has absorbed over decades. That moisture needs a route out, and the route is outward through the masonry.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>vapour-permeable water repellent</strong> lines the pores so liquid water cannot get in, while leaving them open enough for vapour to get out. That is what siloxane and silane-siloxane products do, and it is why they dominate chimney work.
        </p>

        <p>
          A <strong>film-forming sealer</strong> — anything glossy, anything promising a wet look, most cheap acrylic masonry coatings — does the opposite. It stops water entering and stops vapour leaving, so moisture accumulates behind the film. In freezing weather that trapped moisture expands and takes the brick face with it, and the coating peels off carrying spalled brick. The result is a chimney in visibly worse condition than before it was "protected".
        </p>

        <p>
          The practical rule when reading a label: if it mentions gloss, sheen, wet look, or forms a coating, it does not belong on a chimney. If it says vapour permeable, breathable, or penetrating water repellent, you are in the right category. Our <a href="/silane-vs-siloxane-sealers/">silane vs siloxane guide</a> explains how these chemistries differ from one another.
        </p>

        <!-- Product Reviews -->
