        <h2 id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Find the Leak Before You Buy Anything
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sealing brick that was never the problem is the most common wasted afternoon in chimney maintenance. Four checks, from the top down.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Is there a rain cap?</strong> An open flue is a hole in the roof. Rain goes straight down it, into the smoke chamber and the masonry, and no exterior product addresses that. A cap is inexpensive and also keeps birds and debris out.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Is the crown cracked?</strong> The concrete cap on top expands and contracts with every fire and every summer, and hairline cracking is close to universal on older chimneys. Water entering there runs down inside the stack. This is the leak most often blamed on the brick.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Is the flashing intact?</strong> Where the chimney passes through the roof, step flashing and counter-flashing keep water out. Failed sealant, lifted metal or a poor original installation produce leaks that appear at the ceiling and look exactly like a masonry problem.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Is the brick actually absorbing?</strong> Spray a little water on the stack. If it soaks in and darkens the brick, the masonry is porous and a repellent will help. If it beads, either a repellent is still working or the brick is dense — and the leak is coming from somewhere else.
        </p>

        <p>
          Work top-down when repairing too: cap, crown, flashing, then repointing where mortar has eroded, and finally the water repellent. Sealing before repointing simply means doing the sealing twice.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reading the Stains Inside the House</h3>
        <p>
          Water entering a chimney rarely appears where it got in, and the pattern of interior staining is a genuinely useful diagnostic before anyone goes on the roof.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A stain on the ceiling around the chimney breast</strong>, appearing during or shortly after rain, usually means flashing. Water runs down the outside of the stack, meets the roof, and gets in where the two are supposed to be sealed together.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Staining on the chimney breast itself, in the room</strong>, often points at the crown or at saturated masonry. Water travelling down inside the structure carries salts with it, which is why these stains frequently show a white, powdery bloom alongside the discolouration.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Damp appearing days after rain rather than during it</strong> suggests absorption rather than a direct leak — the stack takes up water over hours and releases it slowly inward. That is the pattern a water repellent genuinely fixes.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>A smell of soot when it rains</strong> is water reaching creosote deposits in the flue, which usually means an open flue with no cap. Fix the cap before anything else; it is the cheapest item on the list and the most immediately effective.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. How to Apply It
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Clean and repair first.</strong> The stack needs to be free of soot, algae and loose material, and any spalled brick or eroded mortar dealt with before sealing. A repellent over failing mortar seals in the failure.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Let it dry.</strong> At least 24 hours after cleaning or rain, longer in cool weather. A repellent cannot penetrate pores that are already full of water.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Flood it on, top down.</strong> Chimney repellents are applied generously with a low-pressure sprayer, starting at the top and working down, wet enough that the product runs several inches below the spray line. This is not a paint job — thin, even coverage is wrong here; saturation is what gets the product into the depth of the brick.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Respect the coverage rate.</strong> Manufacturer warranties assume a specified quantity per square foot, and stretching a gallon further than the label allows is the most common reason a treated chimney fails early. Buy generously.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Do the crown separately.</strong> Brush the elastomeric crown coating onto the concrete cap, working it into the cracks. Different product, different technique, same afternoon.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Think about access honestly.</strong> Every product here is easy to apply and none of that matters if the work is thirty feet up on a pitched roof. Roof falls are serious, and a chimney sweep or masonry contractor doing this is buying you their ladders, their harness and their experience as much as their labour.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weather and Timing</h3>
        <p>
          Pick the window carefully, because a chimney gets no shelter from anything. The masonry needs to be dry through before the repellent goes on, which after a wet spell means waiting two or three dry days rather than one — a stack holds water longer than a wall because it is exposed on every face and rarely gets direct sun on all of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Temperature matters at both ends. Most repellents want application above roughly 40°F with no frost forecast overnight, and applying in strong direct sun on a hot day is equally unhelpful, because the product flashes off the surface before it has penetrated. A mild, dry, overcast day in late spring or early autumn is close to ideal, and it also happens to be the most comfortable weather for working on a roof.
        </p>

        <h2 id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Resealing and Upkeep
        </h2>

        <p>
          A quality siloxane repellent typically holds for five to ten years; budget products noticeably less. Rather than counting years, use the same water test each time the roof is being looked at anyway: if water sprayed on the brick soaks in rather than beading, the repellent has worn thin.
        </p>

        <p>
          The rest of chimney upkeep is inspection rather than product. Look at the crown each autumn for new cracking, check the cap is still in place and clear, and watch the flashing after storms. Inside, a stain appearing on the ceiling near the chimney breast is worth investigating immediately rather than at the end of the season — the entry point is almost always above the visible damage.
        </p>

        <p>
          If the chimney is in use, none of this replaces an annual sweep and inspection. A sealer protects the masonry from the outside; the flue liner, the smoke chamber and the draw are a different set of concerns entirely, and they are the ones with safety consequences.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What It Costs Either Way</h3>
        <p>
          Doing the masonry yourself is mostly product cost: a typical residential stack takes a gallon or two of repellent applied to saturation, plus a tub of crown coating. That is a modest sum against the alternative — a spalled chimney eventually needs rebuilding from the damaged course upward, which is scaffolding, a mason and several days of work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Having it done professionally bundles the inspection with the work, which is worth something on its own. A chimney specialist on the roof will see the cap, the crown, the flashing and the mortar in one visit and tell you which of them are actually failing — a diagnosis that is difficult to make convincingly from the ground with binoculars. On an older house where nobody has been on the roof in years, that first inspection often reveals that the sealing everyone was worried about is the least urgent item on the list.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Film-Forming Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Anything glossy or wet-look traps moisture inside the stack, and the next freeze pops the brick faces off. This single mistake causes more chimney damage than leaving the masonry untreated ever would.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing the Brick and Ignoring the Crown</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The crown is where most chimney water gets in. Treating the vertical brick while leaving a cracked horizontal cap open is sealing the walls of a bucket with a hole in its lid.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stretching the Coverage Rate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                These products are applied to saturation, not thinly. Making a gallon go further than the label allows is why a treated chimney can fail within a couple of seasons — and it voids the warranty that justified the price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Over Failing Mortar</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Eroded joints need repointing first. A repellent over crumbling mortar preserves the crumbling, and the repointing then has to be done through a treated surface, which is worse than doing it in the right order.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating the Roof Work</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The products are easy; the access is not. A tall stack on a steep pitch is a genuine fall risk, and it is the part of this job worth paying a professional for even if you buy the materials yourself.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best chimney sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">ChimneySaver Water Repellent is the product chimney specialists most often specify — a vapour-permeable siloxane made for stacks. Ghostshield Siloxa-Tek 8500 is the best general-purpose alternative if other masonry is being treated at the same time, and a brushable crown coating is what actually stops most chimney leaks.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use regular masonry sealer?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Only if it is vapour permeable. A chimney holds moisture that must escape outward, and a film-forming sealer traps it in the brick where it freezes and spalls the faces off. Never use a gloss or wet-look product on a stack.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my chimney brick flaking?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">That is spalling — water inside the brick freezing and popping the face off. The usual causes are an absorbent unsealed stack, a cracked crown letting water in from above, or a missing rain cap. Sealing only helps once those entry points are dealt with.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should it be resealed?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A quality siloxane repellent lasts five to ten years, budget products less. Test rather than count: spray water on the brick, and if it soaks in and darkens the masonry rather than beading, it is time to reapply.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing stop leaks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Only leaks caused by water absorbing through the brick. Water entering through a cracked crown, failed flashing or a missing cap is unaffected by any amount of masonry sealer. Diagnose the entry point before buying anything.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Should the crown be sealed too?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">Yes, with a different product. The crown is horizontal concrete that cracks with thermal movement and needs a flexible elastomeric coating, not a penetrating repellent. Brick and crown are two jobs with two products.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you do it yourself?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">The application is simple — flooded on with a low-pressure sprayer, top down. The difficulty is access: this is work at height on a pitched roof, and if the stack is tall or the roof steep, it is worth paying someone with the ladders and harness to do it.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Will it change how the brick looks?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700">A proper penetrating repellent should not — it soaks in, leaves no film, and the brick simply stops absorbing water. Any product promising a wet look or sheen on a chimney is a film former and the wrong category entirely.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

