        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Use a <strong class="text-white">vapour-permeable siloxane repellent</strong> on the brick — ChimneySaver if you want the specialist product, Siloxa-Tek 8500 if you are treating other masonry too — and a <strong class="text-white">brushable elastomeric coating</strong> on the crown. Never put anything glossy or film-forming on a chimney; it traps moisture and causes exactly the spalling you were trying to prevent.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            And diagnose before you buy. Cap, crown, flashing, mortar, then sealer — in that order. Most chimney leaks blamed on porous brick turn out to be a cracked crown or failed flashing, and sealing the brick beautifully does nothing at all for either.
          </p>
